diagnosis_text:
I checked Location C’s gateway (router4) and the LAN from PC11. The router is up and forwarding. From PC11, the default gateway 192.168.200.1 responds normally (2/2 ICMP, ~0.6 ms). Router4’s interfaces are up with correct IPs: eth0=192.168.200.1/24 (LAN), eth4=192.168.15.2/24, eth5=172.25.1.1/24. The RIP daemon is running (UDP 520 listening) and the FRR config advertises 192.168.200.0/24, 192.168.15.0/24, and 172.25.0.0/16. Router4 has a populated routing table with RIP-learned routes and a default via 172.25.1.2. Router4 can ping both upstream neighbors (192.168.15.1 and 172.25.1.2) with sub-millisecond RTTs. From PC11, traceroute to a remote site host (172.16.10.4) traverses router4 → 192.168.15.1 → 172.20.1.1 and reaches the destination in ~3 ms, confirming intersite connectivity. Additionally, hosts at other sites can reach Location C (e.g., pc4 → pc12 ping succeeds).

Mechanism: If router4 were down, the default gateway would not answer and intersite paths would fail; our tests show the opposite. Localization: router4 processes (RIP) and interfaces eth0/eth4/eth5 are operational.

Root cause: No current fault found on router4; the report likely reflected a transient/local issue that has since cleared or a workstation/switch-port-specific problem.

Corrective fix: No change required on router4. Ask users to retest. If issues persist at specific desks, check the affected PC’s NIC/switch5 port and cabling; otherwise, collect timestamps for any recurrence to correlate with router4 logs. === END ===